requirements.text:
Cybersecurity policies are adopted and documented for at least the following areas:
a) risk management;
b) roles and responsibilities;
c) human resources reliability;
d) compliance and security audits;
e) supply chain cybersecurity risk management;
f) asset management;
g) vulnerability management;
h) business continuity, disaster recovery and cyber crisis management;
i) authentication, digital identity and access control management;
j) physical security;
k) personnel training and awareness;
l) data security;
m) development, configuration, maintenance and decommissioning of network and information systems;
n) protection of networks and communications;
o) security event monitoring;
p) incident response and recovery.

With regard to the award of supply contracts with potential impacts on the security of network and information systems, including through the use of tools provided by central purchasing bodies referred to in Annex I.1, Article 1, paragraph 1, point i), of Legislative Decree No. 36 of 31 March 2023, provision is made for:
a)	the involvement of the cybersecurity organisation referred to in measure GV.RR-02 in defining and implementing procurement processes, starting from the supply identification and design phase;
b)	in accordance with the results of the assessment of the risk associated with the supply referred to in measure GV.SC-07, the definition of security requirements for the supply consistent with the security measures applied by the NIS entity to network and information systems.

For the security requirements referred to in point 1, letter b), at least the following areas are considered, where applicable:
a) reliability of suppliers, taking into account at least any specific vulnerabilities they may have, the overall quality of their products and their cybersecurity practices, especially with regard to the subject matter of the supply, their ability to ensure supply, support and maintenance over time, as well as, where applicable, the results of coordinated assessments of the security risks of critical supply chains carried out by the NIS Cooperation Group;
b) roles and responsibilities within the scope of the supply;
c) reliability of human resources;
d) compliance and security audits;
e) vulnerability management;
f) business continuity and disaster recovery;
g) management of authentication, digital identities and access control;
h) physical security;
i) staff training and awareness;
j) data security;
k) protection of networks and communications;
l) monitoring of security events, including access and activities performed;
m) incident management and reporting;
n) secure code development and security by design and by default;
o) routine and enhancement maintenance, including security updates;
p) termination of the supply, including the return and deletion of data;
q) subcontracting, sub-supply or related potential security requirements along the supply chain.

An up-to-date inventory is maintained of suppliers whose supplies may affect the security of network and information systems, including at least:
a)	the contact details of the supply contact person;
b)	the type of supply.

As part of the risk assessment referred to in measure ID.RA-05, the risk associated with supplies is assessed and documented. To this end, at least the following are assessed:
a) the supplier's level of access to the NIS entity's network and information systems;
b) the supplier's access to intellectual property and data, also based on their criticality;
c) the impact of a severe disruption of supply;
d) recovery times and costs in the event of service unavailability;
e) the supplier's roles and responsibilities in the governance of network and information systems.

The activities referred to in point 2 are documented in dedicated reports containing at least:
a) a general description of the activities performed and their outcomes;
b) a description of the vulnerabilities detected and their associated level of impact on security.

In accordance with the cybersecurity risk management plan referred to in measure GV.RM-03, an assessment of the risk posed to the security of network and information systems is performed and documented, including with reference to any dependencies on third-party suppliers and partners, comprising at least:
a) risk identification;
b) risk analysis;
c) risk evaluation.

A cybersecurity risk treatment plan is defined, documented, implemented and monitored and includes at least:
a) the treatment options and measures to be implemented to treat each identified risk, and their respective priorities;
b) the organizational units responsible for implementing the risk treatment measures and the timelines for their implementation;
c) a description of, and the reasons justifying acceptance of, any residual risks after treatment.

A vulnerability management plan is defined, implemented, updated and documented and includes at least:
a) the methods for identifying the vulnerabilities referred to in measure ID.RA-01 and the related planning of activities;
b) the methods for monitoring, receiving, analysing and responding to information on vulnerabilities;
c) the procedures, roles and responsibilities for carrying out the activities referred to in points a) and b).

For at least the relevant network and information systems, a business continuity plan is defined, implemented, updated and documented, comprising at least:
a) the objectives, including business continuity needs, and the scope of application;
b) the roles and responsibilities;
c) the main contacts and communication channels (internal and external);
d) the conditions for activating and deactivating the plan;
e) the necessary resources, including backups and redundancies.

For at least the relevant network and information systems, a disaster recovery plan is defined, implemented, updated and documented, comprising at least:
a) the purposes, including disaster recovery needs, and the scope of application;
b) roles and responsibilities;
c) key contacts and communication channels (internal and external);
d) the conditions for activating and deactivating the plan;
e) the necessary resources, including backups and redundancies;
f) the order of recovery of operations;
g) recovery procedures for specific operations, including recovery objectives.

For at least the relevant network and information systems, a cyber crisis management plan is defined, implemented, updated and documented, including at least:
a) the roles and responsibilities of personnel and, where appropriate, suppliers, specifying the assignment of roles in crisis situations, including the specific procedures to be followed;
b) the arrangements for communication between entities and competent authorities.

Authentication methods for user accounts to access network and information systems are commensurate with risk. To this end, at least the risks associated with the following are assessed:
a) user account privileges;
b) the criticality of network and information systems;
c) the types of operations that user accounts can perform on network and information systems.

A cybersecurity training plan for personnel, including administrative and management bodies, is defined, implemented, updated and documented, and includes at least:
a)	the planning of the envisaged training activities, indicating the content of the training provided;
b)	any methods for verifying the acquisition of the content.

The plan referred to in measure PR.AT-01 provides for dedicated training for personnel in specialized roles, namely those requiring a set of security-related capabilities and skills, including system administrators, which includes at least:
a)	instructions on the secure configuration and operation of information and network systems;
b)	information on known cyber threats;
c)	instructions on how to behave in the event of security-relevant events.

A plan for managing cybersecurity incidents and notifying CSIRT Italia is defined, implemented, updated and documented, in accordance with Article 25 of the NIS decree, and includes at least:
a)	the stages and procedures for incident management and notification, specifying the relevant roles and responsibilities;
b)	the procedures for preparing and transmitting the reports referred to in Article 25, paragraph 5, points c), d) and e) of the NIS decree;
c)	the contact information for reporting incidents;
d)	the arrangements for internal communication, including with regard to the involvement of administrative and management bodies, and external communication;
e)	the reporting to be used to document the incident.

In accordance with the incident management plan referred to in measure RS.MA-01, procedures are documented and adopted to communicate without undue delay, where deemed appropriate and where possible, after consulting CSIRT Italia, or where ordered by the National Cybersecurity Agency pursuant to Article 37, paragraph 3, points g) and h), of the NIS decree:
a) to the recipients of their services, significant incidents that may adversely affect the provision of those services;
b) to the recipients of their services who are potentially affected by a significant cyber threat, the corrective or mitigation measures or actions that those recipients can take in response to that threat and the nature of that threat.
